<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  plMail's "pl" mark, transcribed from the server's own macro
  (templates/_partials/_logo_mark.html.twig) so the phone and the web wear the
  same logo. Seven strokes in a fixed draw order — p-stem, p-top-curve,
  p-right-stem, p-bowl-bottom, l-upper, l-lower, l-tail — copied verbatim
  rather than redrawn, because a hand-traced logo drifts from the original the
  first time either side changes. It had already drifted: the geometry here was
  a superseded version of the letters.

  The colours are LogoStyle::Berry, the product default and the one the web's
  own PNG icons and fallback favicon are drawn in. What they are NOT is the
  previous set — #4285F4, #34A853, #EA4335, #FBBC04, which are Google's literal
  brand hexes. The web dropped those deliberately (see the docblock on
  LogoStyle, which exists because of it); this icon was still wearing them.

  **The mark now does follow the user's chosen colourway, and this file is
  what it falls back to.** `Appearance` publishes a read-only `logoStyle` over
  JMAP, and the phone swaps activity-aliases to wear it — which is the only way
  an installed Android app's icon can change, and is exactly the mechanism the
  previous note here said would be needed. See LogoStyle and AppLauncherIcon.

  So this file is no longer "the icon": it is the **default** icon, in three
  senses that have to stay the same colourway or the fallbacks stop agreeing
  with each other. It is the application's own `android:icon`, which is what a
  launcher draws for anything that is not one of the aliases. It is what the
  `LogoLauncherBerry` alias points at, rather than that alias getting a
  generated copy of these seven hexes to drift from. And it is where an unknown
  colourway lands — a server newer than this build can name one that shipped
  after it. Berry is the product default on the server and the honest default
  here.

  Repainting this file is therefore repainting the fallback for all thirty-two.
  Changing its *geometry* is a change to all thirty-two: the generator reads the
  strokes out of this file and replaces only their colours, so rerunning
  `tools/generate_launcher_icons.py` is how a correction to the mark reaches
  every colourway.

  ## The arithmetic, for whoever rescales this

  An adaptive-icon foreground is a 108dp canvas whose outer ring is cropped by
  whatever mask the launcher applies; only a circle of radius 33 about the
  centre is guaranteed to survive. The source is a 48-unit square whose inner
  group translates by (-4.5, -0.5) — that is the macro's own transform, kept so
  the paths below are diffable against the twig character for character.

  After that translate the ink spans x 5.75..42.25 and y 3.75..44.25, centred
  exactly on (24, 24). Half-extents are 18.25 and 20.25, so at scale 1.2 the
  furthest corner of the ink's bounding box sits sqrt(21.9² + 24.3²) ≈ 32.7
  from the centre — inside the safe circle, with little to spare. The outer
  group's translate is 54 - 24 × 1.2 = 25.2 on both axes, which is what puts
  the drawing's centre on the canvas's.

  Rescaling means redoing that arithmetic, not nudging the numbers until it
  looks right.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group
        android:scaleX="1.2"
        android:scaleY="1.2"
        android:translateX="25.2"
        android:translateY="25.2">

        <group
            android:translateX="-4.5"
            android:translateY="-0.5">

            <path
                android:fillColor="#00000000"
                android:pathData="M13,11 L13,42"
                android:strokeColor="#6d28d9"
                android:strokeWidth="5.5"
                android:strokeLineCap="round"
                android:strokeLineJoin="round" />

            <path
                android:fillColor="#00000000"
                android:pathData="M13,11 Q13,7 17,7 L23,7 Q29,7 29,13"
                android:strokeColor="#7c3aed"
                android:strokeWidth="5.5"
                android:strokeLineCap="round"
